/-!
# PSL₂(ℤ) is the free product C₂ * C₃

Theorem statement: `ModularGroup.exists_mulEquiv_freeProduct_quotient_center`
(`22fc53a0-d932-55da-98a2-9820eacb2e30`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `15e678a5-1e64-5634-b2c7-ea1a098446f2`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

The assertion is the existence of a group isomorphism $e$ from the free product (coproduct in the
category of groups, `Monoid.CoprodI`) of the family indexed by $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$ whose $i$-th
factor is the group $\mathbb{Z}/(i+2)\mathbb{Z}$ written multiplicatively — so the factors are
cyclic of orders $2$ and $3$ — onto the quotient of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, the group of $2
\times 2$ integer matrices of determinant $1$, by its centre, subject to two normalisations: $e$
sends the canonical image in the free product of the element $1$ of the $0$-th factor
$\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ (transported through `Multiplicative.ofAdd`, i.e. the generator of the
factor of order $2$) to the class modulo the centre of $S = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 &
0\end{pmatrix}$, and sends the canonical image of the element $1$ of the first factor
$\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}$ (the generator of the factor of order $3$) to the class modulo the centre
of $ST = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$. No hypotheses are assumed. The centre of
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ being $\{\pm 1\}$, the quotient is $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$.

This is the classical presentation of the modular group: $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ is the free
product of the cyclic groups generated by the images of $S$ and $ST$, of orders $2$ and $3$. It
underlies the combinatorial analysis of functions on $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$-orbits used in the
numerical work on modular curves, and is cited in the computation of ranks of spaces of additive
maps and of orbit counts for the $S$, $ST$ action.

